  "name": "@draftt-io/draftt-mcp",
  "readme": "# Draftt MCP Server\n\nConnect AI agents to your cloud infrastructure lifecycle data through the Model Context Protocol.\n\n[![npm version](https://img.shields.io/npm/v/@draftt-io/draftt-mcp.svg)](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@draftt-io/draftt-mcp)\n[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-blue.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)\n[![Node.js >= 20](https://img.shields.io/badge/node-%3E%3D20.0.0-brightgreen.svg)](https://nodejs.org/)\n\n[Draftt](https://draftt.io) is the unified control plane for proactive tech stack governance. It provides 360\u00b0 visibility into every component across your cloud estate -- from databases and serverless functions to Kubernetes clusters and container images -- tracking version drift, end-of-life risk, compliance posture, and upgrade planning across AWS, GCP, and Azure. This MCP server connects AI agents directly to the Draftt API, letting you query, analyze, and plan infrastructure upgrades through natural language. All tools are read-only except `draftt_policy_create`, which creates governance policies inside Draftt.\n\n## Features and Use Cases\n\n- **Inventory your entire cloud estate** -- Search and inspect databases, Kubernetes clusters, serverless functions, container images, and every other tracked component across AWS, GCP, and Azure.\n- **Assess compliance and EOL risk** -- Surface which components are past end-of-life or drifting from the latest stable version, with urgency scores and smart prioritization.\n- **Plan Kubernetes upgrades end-to-end** -- Get structured upgrade guides covering control plane, nodes, deprecated APIs, and application-level changes, then inventory every container image and Helm chart running in the cluster.\n- **Quantify extended support costs** -- See the financial impact of running past-EOL components on vendor extended support and prioritize remediation by urgency, effort, and business impact.\n- **Author governance policies from plain English** -- Turn a requirement like \"all RDS instances must have storage encryption enabled\" into a real Draftt policy, with detail fields discovered from the live API schema so rules never reference invented fields.\n- **Guided workflows via prompt templates** -- Use pre-built prompts for compliance reports, K8s upgrade plans, policy creation, and EOL risk assessments that orchestrate multi-tool workflows automatically.\n\n## Quick Start\n\n**1. Get a Draftt API key** at [draftt.io](https://draftt.io)\n\n**2. Run the server with npx:**\n\n```bash\nnpx -y @draftt-io/draftt-mcp --access-key YOUR_ACCESS_KEY\n```\n\n**3. Connect from Claude Desktop** -- add the following to your Claude Desktop configuration file:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"draftt\": {\n      \"command\": \"npx\",\n      \"args\": [\"-y\", \"@draftt-io/draftt-mcp\", \"--access-key\", \"YOUR_ACCESS_KEY\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- **Node.js** 20.0.0 or later ([download](https://nodejs.org/))\n\n## Installation\n\n<details>\n<summary>Claude Desktop</summary>\n\nAdd the following to your Claude Desktop config file.\n\n- **macOS**: `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json`\n- **Windows**: `%APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json`\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"draftt\": {\n      \"command\": \"npx\",\n      \"args\": [\"-y\", \"@draftt-io/draftt-mcp\", \"--access-key\", \"YOUR_ACCESS_KEY\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAlternatively, use an environment variable for the API key:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"draftt\": {\n      \"command\": \"npx\",\n      \"args\": [\"-y\", \"@draftt-io/draftt-mcp\"],\n      \"env\": {\n        \"DRAFTT_ACCESS_KEY\": \"YOUR_ACCESS_KEY\"\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>VS Code / GitHub Copilot</summary>\n\nAdd to your VS Code `settings.json` or workspace `.vscode/settings.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mcp\": {\n    \"servers\": {\n      \"draftt\": {\n        \"command\": \"npx\",\n        \"args\": [\"-y\", \"@draftt-io/draftt-mcp\", \"--access-key\", \"YOUR_ACCESS_KEY\"]\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Cursor</summary>\n\nAdd to your Cursor MCP configuration file (`.cursor/mcp.json`):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"draftt\": {\n      \"command\": \"npx\",\n      \"args\": [\"-y\", \"@draftt-io/draftt-mcp\", \"--access-key\", \"YOUR_ACCESS_KEY\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Claude Code CLI</summary>\n\n```bash\nclaude mcp add draftt -- npx -y @draftt-io/draftt-mcp --access-key YOUR_ACCESS_KEY\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Kiro IDE</summary>\n\nAdd to your Kiro MCP configuration file (`.kiro/settings/mcp.json`).\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"draftt\": {\n      \"command\": \"npx\",\n      \"args\": [\"-y\", \"@draftt-io/draftt-mcp\", \"--access-key\", \"YOUR_ACCESS_KEY\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>HTTP / SSE Transport</summary>\n\nFor web-based or remote clients, start the Streamable HTTP transport:\n\n```bash\nDRAFTT_ACCESS_KEY=YOUR_ACCESS_KEY npm run start:http\n```\n\nThe server exposes a single `/mcp` endpoint on port 3000 (configurable via `PORT`). Clients connect with `POST /mcp` (initialize and send messages), `GET /mcp` (SSE stream), and `DELETE /mcp` (terminate session). A `/health` endpoint is also available for monitoring.\n\nSessions are automatically cleaned up after 30 minutes of inactivity. CORS and DNS rebinding protection are configurable via `ALLOWED_ORIGINS`.\n\n</details>\n\n## Configuration\n\n| Name | Required | Default | Description |\n|------|----------|---------|-------------|\n| `DRAFTT_ACCESS_KEY` | Yes | -- | API authentication key. Also available as `--access-key` CLI flag. |\n| `DRAFTT_API_BASE_URL` | No | `https://api.draftt.io/v1` | Draftt API base URL. |\n| `MCP_TRANSPORT` | No | `stdio` | Transport mode: `stdio` or `sse`. |\n| `PORT` | No | `3000` | HTTP server port (HTTP transport only). |\n| `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` | No | `*` | Comma-separated list of allowed CORS origins (HTTP transport only). |\n| `DEBUG` | No | `false` | Enable verbose debug logging. Also available as `--debug` CLI flag. |\n| `DRAFTT_ALLOW_UNCONFIRMED_CREATE` | No | `false` | Bypass the confirmation flow entirely for all clients, allowing `draftt_policy_create` to run without user approval. See [Creating Policies](#creating-policies). |\n\n## Available Tools\n\n### Component Management\n\n| Tool | Description | Key Parameters |\n|------|-------------|----------------|\n| `draftt_component_query` | Query cloud infrastructure components with DrafttQL (`$eq`, `$ne`, `$gt`, `$lt`, `$in`, `$nin`, `$and`, `$or`). Filter by tags with `tags$.<tagKey>` / `customTags$.<tagKey>` keys in `filter`. Supports field selection, sorting, and cursor pagination up to 1,000 results. | `filter`, `select`, `sort`, `pageSize`, `cursor` |\n| `draftt_component_get` | Get full details for a single component by numeric ID, including version, region, ARN, and tags. | `componentId` |\n| `draftt_component_details_schema` | Discover the queryable `details$.` fields for a technology (or technology group), with each field's path, display name, type, and enum `availableValues`. Detail fields are not listed in any resource -- call this before creating a component policy that checks details. | `technology` |\n\n### Policy and Compliance\n\n| Tool | Description | Key Parameters |\n|------|-------------|----------------|\n| `draftt_policy_query` | Query compliance policies with DrafttQL filtering, field selection, sorting, and cursor pagination. EOL policies flag end-of-life; L&G policies track latest version. | `filter`, `select`, `sort`, `pageSize`, `cursor` |\n| `draftt_policy_get` | Get full details for a single policy by numeric ID. | `policyId` |\n| `draftt_policy_create` | **Write operation -- always requires user confirmation.** Create a policy that evaluates resources for compliance. Supports resources `component`, `certificate` and types `governance`, `lifecycle`. Every referenced `details$.` field is verified against the live API schema; the create is rejected if a field does not exist. See [Creating Policies](#creating-policies). | `name`, `description`, `type`, `resource`, `query.filter`, `statusDefinitions` |\n| `draftt_policy_component_get` | Get compliance evaluation of one component against one policy -- compliance status, version gap (current vs required/recommended version), urgency, priority, and due date. | `policyId`, `componentId` |\n| `draftt_policy_component_query` | Query all component compliance evaluations within a policy. Supports filtering, field selection, sorting, tag filtering, and pagination up to 1,000. | `policyId`, `filter`, `tagFilters`, `select`, `sort`, `pageSize` |\n\n### Upgrade Plans\n\n| Tool | Description | Key Parameters |\n|------|-------------|----------------|\n| `draftt_query` | Query upgrade plans (draftts) across the organization. Returns active and completed plans with component ID, technology, current/desired versions, and status. For one component's detailed guide, use `draftt_upgrade_guide_get`. | `filter`, `select`, `sort`, `pageSize`, `cursor` |\n\n### Kubernetes\n\n| Tool | Description | Key Parameters |\n|------|-------------|----------------|\n| `draftt_upgrade_guide_get` | Get a structured K8s cluster upgrade guide by `componentId`. Returns 4 sections: `k8sApi`, `deployments`, `nodes`, `controlPlane`. Recommended execution order: k8sApi \u2192 deployments \u2192 nodes \u2192 controlPlane. Post-upgrade node roll to match control plane is optional, not required. | `componentId`, `sections` |\n| `draftt_policy_k8s_component_query` | Query workload compliance (container images, Helm charts) inside K8s clusters. Returns compliance status, versions, image details, namespace, required action, warnings, Helm/ArgoCD management, and chart upgrade data. Filter by `componentId` to scope to a cluster. Uses a dedicated K8s policy automatically. | `filter`, `k8sCatalogComponentData`, `select`, `sort`, `pageSize` |\n| `draftt_k8s_api_query` | Query K8s API resources (CRDs and native APIs) for a cluster. Returns compliance status, current/desired API versions, kind, CRD origin component, CRD management (Helm/ArgoCD), and deprecated resource instances with migration details. | `componentId`, `filter`, `select`, `sort`, `pageSize` |\n| `draftt_k8s_node_query` | Query K8s nodes for a cluster. Returns compliance status, current/desired kubelet versions, node name, OS image, node group, provider, management info (provisioner type, capacity type), runtime info (kernel, container runtime, architecture), and upgrade warnings. `nodeManagement.provider=\"unknown\"` means the node is not managed (no node group or Karpenter). | `componentId`, `filter`, `select`, `sort`, `pageSize` |\n\n## Resources\n\n| Name | URI | Description |\n|------|-----|-------------|\n| API Configuration | `draftt://config/api` | Current API endpoint configuration and available routes. |\n| Supported Technologies | `draftt://config/technologies` | Reference data for technologies, component types, and cloud regions (not exhaustive). |\n| Domain Glossary | `draftt://guide/concepts` | Definitions of key Draftt concepts: components, policies, compliance, DrafttQL. |\n| Common Workflows | `draftt://guide/workflows` | Step-by-step guides for component discovery, compliance assessment, K8s upgrade planning, and EOL analysis. |\n| Component Policy Filter Fields | `draftt://policy/fields/component` | Base (top-level) filter fields for component policies, with types and valid operators. Does **not** enumerate `details$.` fields -- discover those with `draftt_component_details_schema`. |\n| Certificate Policy Filter Fields | `draftt://policy/fields/certificate` | All filter fields available for certificate policies. Read before creating a certificate policy. |\n| DrafttQL Policy Operators | `draftt://policy/operators` | Complete operator reference for policy filter conditions, including syntax rules, relative date tokens, and common patterns. |\n| Component Details | `draftt://components/{id}` | Dynamic template: full details for a specific component by ID. |\n| Policy Details | `draftt://policies/{id}` | Dynamic template: full details for a specific policy by ID. |\n\n## Prompt Templates\n\n| Name | Description | Arguments |\n|------|-------------|-----------|\n| `compliance-report` | Generate a compliance report for a policy. Summarizes compliant vs. non-compliant counts, highlights highest-urgency items, and recommends remediation priorities. | `policyId` (required), `focusTechnology` (optional) |\n| `k8s-upgrade-plan` | Kubernetes upgrade planning workflow. Discovers the cluster, retrieves the upgrade guide, explores the user's code repo for deployment configs, and produces a concrete implementation plan with file paths, commands, and per-task risk/effort notes. | `componentId` (optional), `clusterName` (optional) |\n| `policy-creation` | Guide for creating a policy from a compliance requirement. Translates natural language into a policy structure with scope, statuses, and filter-based rules, referencing the policy field resources for available fields and operators. | `requirement` (required), `resource` (optional), `type` (optional) |\n| `eol-assessment` | Assess end-of-life risk across all components of a technology. Categorizes by urgency, calculates total extended support costs, and recommends upgrade paths. | `technology` (required) |\n\n## Creating Policies\n\n`draftt_policy_create` is the only write tool. A policy has a **scope** (which resources it applies to) and **status definitions** (how each in-scope resource is classified).\n\n- **Scope** (`query.filter`) -- a DrafttQL filter selecting the resources the policy evaluates, e.g. `{ \"technology\": { \"$eq\": \"rds\" } }`. Use `{}` (or omit) for all resources of the type. Don't repeat scope conditions inside rules.\n- **Status definitions** -- a map of status name to definition. Exactly one status must be the default (`isDefault: true`, no rules); every other status needs `rules`. `criticality` must be unique per status (convention: Non-Compliant `20`, Compliant `10`).\n- **Rules and conditions** -- multiple rules are OR-ed (matching any one is enough); conditions within a rule are AND-ed. Each condition checks a single field.\n- **Prerequisites** -- optional per-rule filters for multi-segment policies (\"if lambda then X, if rds then Y\"). Resources matching no prerequisite become `unknown`.\n- **Operators** -- `$eq`, `$ne`, `$gt`, `$gte`, `$lt`, `$lte`, `$in`, `$nin`, `$exists`, `$ilike`, `$containsAny`. There is no `$or`: use `$in` for alternatives on the same field, or multiple rules for cross-field OR.\n- **Field notation** -- `tags$.<key>` for tags, `details$.<fieldName>` for indexed component details.\n- **Dates** -- ISO strings for absolute dates, or a relative token: `$__draftt__$:relativeDate$:{\"amount\":30,\"unit\":\"days\",\"direction\":\"ahead\"}`.\n\n### Required workflow\n\n1. Identify the resource type and technology from the requirement (\"RDS must have storage encryption\" \u2192 resource `component`, technology `rds`).\n2. If the rules check component details, call `draftt_component_details_schema` for that technology **first** and copy the returned `details$.` paths verbatim. Detail fields are not listed in any resource -- the API schema is the only source of truth, so never guess a path.\n3. Read `draftt://policy/fields/component` for base fields and tags (or `draftt://policy/fields/certificate` for certificates), and `draftt://policy/operators` for operator syntax.\n4. Call `draftt_policy_create`.\n\n### User confirmation\n\n`draftt_policy_create` always asks the user to confirm before it sends anything to the API, showing the exact JSON payload that will be submitted so the rules and conditions can be reviewed. The prompt is issued by the **server** via MCP [elicitation](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/server/elicitation), not by the client's tool-approval flow, so a client that auto-approves tool calls (or has the tool allow-listed) still cannot create a policy silently.\n\nThe gate fails closed on an explicit no: a decline, a 2-minute timeout, or a transport error blocks the create and returns an error explaining that nothing was created.\n\nSome clients can't show the prompt at all -- they don't advertise elicitation, or they advertise it but auto-cancel every prompt (headless agents such as `claude -p`). In that case the tool returns `CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED` (an intermediate step, not an error) with a summary of what would be created and a single-use `confirmationToken` valid for 5 minutes. The agent must include the summary, `confirmationToken`, and `expiresAt` verbatim in its reply to the user -- so approval can happen later, or from another session -- and, only after explicit approval, call the tool again with the same arguments plus the token. The token is bound to the exact payload: any change to the arguments, reuse, or expiry yields `CONFIRMATION_TOKEN_INVALID` and the flow starts over.\n\n| Result | Code |\n|--------|------|\n| User declined | `CONFIRMATION_DECLINED` |\n| Prompt timed out or failed | `CONFIRMATION_FAILED` |\n| Prompt cancelled or client lacks elicitation -- token issued | `CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED` |\n| Token unknown, expired, already used, or arguments changed | `CONFIRMATION_TOKEN_INVALID` |\n\nSet `DRAFTT_ALLOW_UNCONFIRMED_CREATE=true` to bypass the confirmation flow entirely \u2014 no elicitation prompt, no confirmation token. When set, policy creates proceed immediately without requiring user approval.\n\nIf you want the confirmation prompt on top of your client's own approval step, keep the tool out of your client's allow-list. In Claude Code, add it to `ask` in `.claude/settings.json`:\n\n```json\n{ \"permissions\": { \"ask\": [\"mcp__draftt__draftt_policy_create\"] } }\n```\n\n### Field validation\n\nThe tool validates every referenced `details$.` field against the live schema and rejects the request without creating anything if a field doesn't exist. If Draftt doesn't track the requested property, the policy cannot be created -- substituting a different field would produce a policy whose name doesn't match what it evaluates.\n\n### Example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"RDS Storage Encryption\",\n  \"description\": \"All RDS instances must have storage encryption enabled.\",\n  \"type\": \"governance\",\n  \"resource\": \"component\",\n  \"query\": { \"filter\": { \"technology\": { \"$eq\": \"rds\" } } },\n  \"statusDefinitions\": {\n    \"Compliant\": {\n      \"criticality\": 10,\n      \"color\": \"#7083CE\",\n      \"rules\": [\n        {\n          \"conditions\": [\n            {\n              \"type\": \"filter\",\n              \"condition\": { \"filter\": { \"details$.rds$.storageEncrypted\": { \"$eq\": true } } }\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"Non-Compliant\": {\n      \"criticality\": 20,\n      \"color\": \"#FF4747\",\n      \"isDefault\": true\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Example Queries\n\n### Component Discovery\n\n- \"Show me all RDS PostgreSQL databases in us-east-1\"\n- \"List every EKS cluster tagged with environment=production\"\n- \"What Lambda functions are running Node.js 16?\"\n\n### Compliance and EOL\n\n- \"Which components are non-compliant with the End of Life policy?\"\n- \"Show me the 10 most urgent RDS upgrades sorted by extended support cost\"\n- \"Generate a compliance report for policy 1 focused on elasticache\"\n- \"How much are we spending on extended support for past-EOL components?\"\n\n### Kubernetes Upgrades\n\n- \"Get the full upgrade guide for EKS cluster 42\"\n- \"What container images are running in our production GKE cluster?\"\n- \"Create a K8s upgrade plan for our cluster named staging-eks-01\"\n\n### Policy Creation\n\n- \"Create a policy that requires all RDS instances to have storage encryption enabled\"\n- \"What detail fields can I write policy rules against for lambda?\"\n- \"Add a governance policy flagging TLS certificates that expire within 30 days\"\n\n## Security\n\n- **Dedicated API keys** -- Each environment or team should use its own `DRAFTT_ACCESS_KEY`. Never share keys across security boundaries.\n- **Environment variables for secrets** -- Store `DRAFTT_ACCESS_KEY` in environment variables or a secrets manager. Avoid committing keys to source control. The `--access-key` CLI flag is provided as a convenience for local development.\n- **Key rotation** -- Rotate API keys periodically through the Draftt dashboard. The server picks up new keys on restart.\n- **Read-only by default** -- Every tool except `draftt_policy_create` is read-only and idempotent. `draftt_policy_create` writes only Draftt policy metadata; it is non-destructive and never creates, updates, or deletes resources in your cloud accounts. No tool ever mutates your cloud infrastructure.\n- **Server-enforced confirmation for writes** -- `draftt_policy_create` prompts the user via MCP elicitation before every create, enforced server-side so a client that auto-approves tool calls cannot bypass it. It fails closed on decline, timeout, or error. See [User confirmation](#user-confirmation).\n- **Transport security** -- When using the HTTP transport in production, deploy behind a TLS-terminating reverse proxy. Configure `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` to restrict cross-origin access and enable DNS rebinding protection.\n\n## Development\n\n### Prerequisites\n\n- Node.js >= 20.0.0\n- npm\n\n### Scripts\n\n```bash\n# Install dependencies\nnpm install\n\n# Build TypeScript to JavaScript\nnpm run build\n\n# Start STDIO transport server\nnpm start\n\n# Start HTTP/SSE transport server\nnpm run start:http\n\n# Development mode (ts-node)\nnpm run dev\n\n# Run tests\nnpm test\n\n# Run tests with coverage\nnpm run test:coverage\n\n# Lint\nnpx eslint src/\n\n# Auto-fix lint issues\nnpx eslint src/ --fix\n```\n\n## License\n\n[MIT](LICENSE)\n",
